# CI/CD rules

CI files are protected. The agent **proposes** CI changes as a patch file in `.agent/work/F-NNN/ci.patch` with a rationale. A human applies it.

## What a healthy pipeline has (audit against this list)
- A matrix that covers macOS (arm64, `macos-latest`), Windows (MSVC, `windows-latest`) and Linux (GCC + Clang), with Debug and Release.
- Warnings as errors on at least one strict job (`-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion`, `/W4 /permissive-`).
- Sanitizer jobs (ASan+UBSan, TSan where threads are used).
- The `hpcgate check` correctness stages on every PR. Perf on shared runners only in `--perf-advisory` mode. Blocking perf only on a dedicated or self-hosted runner with fixed CPU frequency.
- A reproducible toolchain: pinned action versions (by SHA), pinned compiler/CMake versions, `CMakePresets.json` shared by CI and developers.
- Caching (ccache/sccache, dependency caches) keyed on compiler + lockfiles. A cache must never change results.
- Fast feedback: format/lint/guard checks first (seconds), full matrix after.
- Artifacts: gate reports and test logs uploaded on failure.
- Least privilege: `permissions: contents: read` by default, no secrets in PR jobs from forks.

## Findings in this category
Name the failure the gap allows: "a Windows-only overflow in `long` cannot be caught because no MSVC job exists", not "should add Windows CI".
